<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Statielli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Statellates</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Statielli were a Ligurian people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, around Aquae Statiellae, modern Acqui Terme. In 173 BCE the consul Marcus Popillius Laenas attacked them although they had not taken up arms and sold the survivors into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; the Senate condemned his conduct and ordered their freedom restored. They later became part of Augustus's Ligurian region.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The people are named only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, in his account of the events of 173–172 BCE, as Statellates and Ligures Statellates; inscriptions of the Roman period give the form Statiellenses. Both forms carry suffixes common in Ligurian names. Petracco Sicardi derives them from a base *stat-io, and the name has been linked to an Indo-European root meaning to stand or stay, in the sense of the natives or those who hold a territory. Like the other small peoples of the region, the Statielli are conventionally called Celto-Ligurian, a label of Hellenistic Greek origin.</p><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> the inland district of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> belonged to a wider Ligurian cultural area, and the Statielli become recognisable as a group only from about the 4th century BCE. How these peoples formed is disputed: Ermanno Arslan argues for a largely local development from a Ligurian substrate, against the view that Golaseccan Celtic bands pushed the Ligurians back onto the Apennine ridges in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 173 BCE the consul Marcus Popillius Laenas received the surrender of the Statielli and their stronghold Carystum, then put many to death and destroyed the town. In 172 BCE, after the Senate ordered the enslaved to be freed, the community was resettled north of the Po, and finds at Palazzolo in the lower Vercellese have been connected with the displaced group. When the Via Postumia was laid out in 148 BCE from Genua to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> by way of the Scrivia valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> first had to secure the upland peoples who held the crossing, the Statielli among them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Their land lay in the upper basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormida">Bormida</a>, between the valleys of the Bormida di Millesimo and the Orba, with the Bagienni to the west; Arslan extends it east to the Scrivia valley. Carystum has not been located, though it is generally placed at or near Acqui Terme. A cremation cemetery of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> has been excavated at Montabone. Arslan describes their culture as one of incomplete Laténization: La Tène objects appear only now and then over a Ligurian tradition, whose locally made impasto pottery goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Statielli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They lived as separate tribes, among them the Ingauni, Intimilii, Apuani and <b>Statielli</b>, in hill forts and upland villages, combining herding, small-scale farming and raiding.</blockquote><a href="/state/ligures">Ligures</a></li></ul></section>
